WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.800
EfficientNet.

2
00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:05.640
When you have more compute to spend, how should a CNN grow?

3
00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:07.800
This model gave a principled answer.

4
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.267
Given more compute, you have three knobs.

5
00:00:11.567 --> 00:00:13.233
Go deeper, with more layers.

6
00:00:13.533 --> 00:00:15.333
Go wider, with more channels.

7
00:00:15.633 --> 00:00:17.717
Or feed higher resolution images.

8
00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:19.800
Teams used to pick one and guess.

9
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.700
EfficientNet's insight is that these axes depend on each other.

10
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:31.333
Higher resolution needs more layers to see the larger region, and more channels to capture finer detail.

11
00:00:31.633 --> 00:00:33.233
So scale all three together.

12
00:00:34.433 --> 00:00:36.800
Here is the rule, called compound scaling.

13
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:39.833
Depth is multiplied by alpha raised to phi.

14
00:00:40.133 --> 00:00:41.967
Width by beta raised to phi.

15
00:00:42.267 --> 00:00:44.782
And resolution by gamma raised to phi.

16
00:00:44.782 --> 00:00:51.733
You grid search alpha, beta and gamma once on a small model, then turn phi to get B zero through B seven.

17
00:00:52.933 --> 00:00:59.000
Paired with a mobile friendly building block, this was state of the art at about ten times fewer parameters.

18
00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:08.500
B zero has five point three million parameters, against eleven point seven for ResNet-18, and it is typically a couple of points more accurate.

19
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:11.933
Fine-tuning it takes only a few lines.

20
00:01:12.233 --> 00:01:14.333
We load the pretrained B zero model.

21
00:01:14.633 --> 00:01:18.400
Then replace the final layer with one that outputs two classes.

22
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:21.867
Count the parameters, and we get five point three million.

23
00:01:23.067 --> 00:01:25.133
Here is why this matters in production.

24
00:01:25.433 --> 00:01:31.000
A pipeline classifies forty thousand satellite tiles nightly on a single cheap GPU.

25
00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:37.567
Swapping ResNet-50 for EfficientNet-B1 costs only four tenths of a point of accuracy.

26
00:01:37.867 --> 00:01:41.080
The batch drops from sixty five minutes to twenty two.

27
00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:43.400
Accuracy per FLOP is a business metric.

28
00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:45.188
To recap.

29
00:01:45.188 --> 00:01:47.933
Grow depth, width and resolution together.

30
00:01:48.233 --> 00:01:50.267
One knob, phi, sets the budget.

31
00:01:50.567 --> 00:01:53.867
B zero is less than half the size of ResNet-18.

32
00:01:54.167 --> 00:01:56.227
Use it when GPU cost matters.

33
00:01:56.227 --> 00:01:58.500
Next up: the Vision Transformer.
